<!--
    Kuna pushan: P2 flow-follow, option pushan (default auto).
    The script loads the synthetic static x86-64 ELF
    decompiler/crates/kuna-analysis/tests/fixtures/pushan_vm_x86_64
    (generator pushan_vm_x86_64.py): `func` is a protector's entry stub,
    `push 0x5a17; jmp vm_entry`, into a stack-machine interpreter that has
    its own 64 KiB executable section .vmp0.  Its handlers are dispatched by
    `push [handlers+op*8]; ret` and its bytecode sits in a high-entropy block,
    encoding `return arg0 == 0 ? 7 : arg0 + 5` with a JZ that selects the
    next virtual PC by cmov.

    Pass 1 (`option pushan off`) is the bug: the stub's jump into the
    interpreter is all the flow follower sees, and `func` decompiles to an
    empty body (assert #1) with no trace of the protected program.
    Pass 2 (the default, auto) detects the jump into the foreign VM section,
    explores the interpreter keyed by native address and virtual PC, forks on
    the cmov-selected virtual PC, and lifts the recovered program from the
    synthetic p-code overlay: the header warning (assert #2), the virtual
    argument as a parameter (#3), the virtual branch as a test of it (#4)
    and both virtual returns (#5, #6).
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x100000" readonly="true">
c3
</bytechunk>
